      "id": 77304240,
      "body": "I actually planned on giving the post a shout out this week, not because I advocate for the mindset that might be implicit in his words—but most of what he says about optics is true. \n\nFor example, when he said, “whether you like it or not/however you feel about it, it’s how it is.”\n\nEven if it’s not the world we want, he’s telling us the world we have. We can do a separate set of things to change and influence that, and choose to do the right thing, but it’s important to understand the environment you’re in, because a lot of what he says is unspoken and you’re left to realize it through experience. \n\nTo your point, I agree we shouldn’t solely optimize for optics, but it didn’t seem like he was arguing for people to do that either. It seemed like he was arguing for not being overly dogmatic on a set of idealistic “rules of engineering” and to instead be more pragmatic, realizing it’s just about understanding what your leadership cares about as your users",
      "body_json": {
        "type": "doc",
        "attrs": {
          "schemaVersion": "v1"
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "I actually planned on giving the post a shout out this week, not because I advocate for the mindset that might be implicit in his words—but most of what he says about optics is true."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "For example, when he said, “whether you like it or not/however you feel about it, it’s how it is.”"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Even if it’s not the world we want, he’s telling us the world we have. We can do a separate set of things to change and influence that, and choose to do the right thing, but it’s important to understand the environment you’re in, because a lot of what he says is unspoken and you’re left to realize it through experience."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "To your point, I agree we shouldn’t solely optimize for optics, but it didn’t seem like he was arguing for people to do that either. It seemed like he was arguing for not being overly dogmatic on a set of idealistic “rules of engineering” and to instead be more pragmatic, realizing it’s just about understanding what your leadership cares about as your users"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
